From d92ac908d4c5018ddb612f394c4e880abc0057f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 04:20:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gl: scale the sample-grid tie bias with the internal scale

The alignment shift is half a HIRES pixel (0.5/S in native units), and it is
what makes GL's pixel-center sampling agree with the PS1 DDA's integer grid.
The 1/64 backoff that keeps edges off exact sample centers was written as a
constant in NATIVE pixels, so it did not shrink with the shift it corrects:
at S=4 it consumed 12.5% of the shift, at S=16 fully 50%, and at S>=32 it
drove the shift negative.

With the alignment half-destroyed, two polygons sharing an edge rasterize it
on opposite sides of the sample grid and neither covers the boundary column -
a hairline of whatever is behind, at every seam in the mesh. On GT2 that is
the blue/black lines through the track surface and skybox, and it got much
worse when supersampling was raised from 4x to 16x.

The tie being avoided is a hires sample-center tie, so the bias belongs in
hires units too. Bit-identical at S=1; restores ~97% of the ideal shift at
every scale instead of decaying to 50% at 16x.
---
 runtime/src/gpu_gl_renderer.c | 23 ++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/runtime/src/gpu_gl_renderer.c b/runtime/src/gpu_gl_renderer.c
index 1fe18f6e..5b117cd4 100644
--- a/runtime/src/gpu_gl_renderer.c
+++ b/runtime/src/gpu_gl_renderer.c
@@ -3425,14 +3425,27 @@ static int init_gpu_raster(void) {
     p_glUniform1f(s_tex_uXscale, 1.0f); p_glUniform1f(s_tex_uXcenter, 0.0f);
 
     /* Sample-grid alignment shift: half an HR pixel, set once (S is fixed
-     * for the lifetime of the pipeline). Backed off by 1/64 native px so
+     * for the lifetime of the pipeline). Backed off by 1/64 of an HR pixel so
      * primitive edges never land EXACTLY on sample centers — that float tie
      * dropped 1px columns at quad seams (e.g. the 256px texture-page seam in
-     * Tomba's title background). The 1/64 bias keeps floor(uv) on the exact
-     * PS1 texel for |uv slope| < 64; mirrored (negative-slope) mappings can
-     * be off by one texel at exact-integer uv — accepted. */
+     * Tomba's title background). The bias keeps floor(uv) on the exact PS1
+     * texel for |uv slope| < 64; mirrored (negative-slope) mappings can be off
+     * by one texel at exact-integer uv — accepted.
+     *
+     * BOTH terms must scale with S. The tie this backs off from is a HIRES
+     * sample-center tie, so the bias is a fraction of an HR pixel, not of a
+     * native one. Written as a constant 1/64 NATIVE px it stayed fixed while
+     * the shift it corrects shrank as 0.5/S: at S=4 it ate 12% of the shift,
+     * at S=16 fully 50%, and at S>=32 it flipped the shift negative. A
+     * half-destroyed alignment is exactly the failure the shift exists to
+     * prevent — GL samples at pixel centers, the PS1 DDA at integer coords —
+     * so adjacent polygons rasterized their shared edge on opposite sides of
+     * the sample grid and left a hairline of background at every seam
+     * (reported on GT2 as blue/black lines through track and skybox, and it
+     * got visibly worse the moment supersampling was raised from 4x to 16x).
+     * At S=1 this is bit-identical to the old expression. */
     {
-        float shift = 0.5f / (float)s_scale - 1.0f / 64.0f;
+        float shift = (0.5f - 1.0f / 64.0f) / (float)s_scale;
         p_glUseProgram(s_geo_prog);
         p_glUniform1f(p_glGetUniformLocation(s_geo_prog, "u_shift"), shift);
         /* Native-wide projection defaults: x translation 0, clip half-extent
